Interview Tips — Day 7
Q: How does a neural network learn?

"Through forward pass and backpropagation. Data flows forward through layers making a prediction. The loss function measures how wrong that prediction was. Backpropagation calculates each weight's contribution to the error. Gradient descent then adjusts all weights to reduce error. This repeats thousands of times until the model converges."

Q: Why do we need activation functions?

"Activation functions add non-linearity to the network. Without them, no matter how many layers you stack, the network collapses into a single linear equation. Non-linearity allows the network to learn complex patterns like images, speech and sentiment — things a straight line could never capture."

Q: What is the difference between an epoch and a forward pass?

"A forward pass is one prediction for one data point. An epoch is one complete pass through the entire training dataset — meaning all forward passes + backpropagation for every training example. Training for 100 epochs means the model saw every training example 100 times."
